id: recipe.cache-backed-api
kind: recipe
title: Cache-backed API
aliases: cache backed api, cached reads, redis in front of database
question: how would you design an API that serves hot reads from a cache?
tags: architecture, caching, performance
properties:
  - read latency decoupled from the database
  - bounded staleness (TTL + invalidation)
  - stampede protection on hot keys
  - graceful degradation when the cache is down
invariant: The database stays the source of truth; the cache is derived, bounded by TTL and invalidated on write.
answer_30s: Read the cache first with a TTL, fall back to the database on miss, invalidate on write, single-flight the misses for hot keys, and keep working when the cache is unavailable.
flow: |
  Client -> API
     |
  Redis (hit -> return)          miss
     |                            |
     |                        Database -> populate cache with TTL
     |
  write path: database -> invalidate key (never update in place)
compose:
  - design.cache-aside => the read path and its fallback are explicit
  - design.cache-invalidation => TTL plus explicit invalidation bounds staleness
  - design.redis-cache => the store's failure modes (eviction, memory, latency) are known
  - design.timeouts => a cache or database call that hangs must not hold the request open
  - design.backpressure => a cold cache must not turn into a database stampede
  - design.connection-pool => database load is bounded even on cache miss storms
  - design.observability => hit ratio and miss latency are the two numbers to watch
  - k8s.service => stable name for the cache
  - rust.app-state => the cache client is shared, not per request
  - rust.tokio-semaphore => caps concurrent database fills
failure_modes:
  - no TTL, so a missed invalidation is permanent
  - cache treated as authoritative, so an eviction becomes a data loss incident
  - stampede on restart: every instance misses simultaneously and the database falls over
  - inconsistent hash keys, so the same logical value is cached once per service
follow_ups:
  - how do you keep 20 replicas' caches consistent after a write?
related:
  - design.cache-aside
  - design.redis-cache
  - recipe.database-backed-api
sources:
  - https://redis.io/docs/manual/patterns/
